This example shows how to obtain access to the internal type
tables and/or pointers hidden by the compiler.
The example works with a non-trivial inheritance structure
which contains a virtual base class Object.

There is practically no computation. The program creates one
object of type Image, and shows all the tricks you
can do with the types.

Part 1 shows how to detect the true type of the object, even
if you have a sub-type pointer. It also shows how to access
the type information stored in structure zzTypeInfo.

Part 2 iterates through all subtypes (both inherited and member
types), as you would typically do when coding your own class browser.

Part 3 traverses data structure pointers (gives values and target types),
which are transparently generated by the Code Farms library, and 
inserted under the ZZ_EXT_.. statements. These are the pointers that
form the linked lists, trees, and other data structures.
You may need this information if you decide to code your own persistent data.

Part 4 shows how to access masks that lead to the locations and
values of the virtual pointers, usually hidden by the C++ compilers.
This information is useful when coding persistent data, or in any
other case when you are concerned about the internal composition of
objects. Note that our library uses a smart algorithm which
is independent of the internal logic of the compiler.

The type info is kept in the following structure:

struct zzTypeInfo {  // structure which keeps the type info
    char *name;  // class name
    int size;    // size of this class
    char *mask1; // 0-filled object with correct v.f/v.c. pointers
    char *mask2; // 'F' or 'C' for bytes that start v.f./v.c. pointers
    int virt;    // bin packed:
        // 01=abstr.class,02=virt.base class,04=virt.functions present
};

In order to invoke the program, just type:  test
There is no input data.
